1
00:00:01,880 --> 00:00:02,980
Most people learning Gen

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:04,940
AI stop at prompting.

3
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:06,460
But it's a full stack,

4
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:08,073
from tokens to agents,

5
00:00:08,093 --> 00:00:10,420
and the last eight are where the jobs are.

6
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:13,320
Here are the twenty you actually need.

7
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:15,280
Hi, I'm Chris.

8
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:16,680
I've spent over 10 years

9
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:17,640
in data engineering

10
00:00:17,660 --> 00:00:20,520
and interviewed more than 200 engineers.

11
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:22,280
Now I run Stackle, where I teach

12
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:24,440
the skills that actually get you hired.

13
00:00:24,460 --> 00:00:25,980
Let's get into it.

14
00:00:26,437 --> 00:00:28,284
One: tokens.

15
00:00:28,304 --> 00:00:30,017
Models read text in small pieces

16
00:00:30,037 --> 00:00:32,817
called tokens, and you pay per token.

17
00:00:32,837 --> 00:00:34,417
Two: embeddings.

18
00:00:34,437 --> 00:00:36,177
Text becomes a list of numbers,

19
00:00:36,197 --> 00:00:38,657
so similar meanings sit close together.

20
00:00:38,677 --> 00:00:40,337
Three: attention.

21
00:00:40,357 --> 00:00:42,177
Every token looks at every other

22
00:00:42,197 --> 00:00:44,257
token to work out what it means.

23
00:00:44,277 --> 00:00:46,364
Four: large language models.

24
00:00:46,384 --> 00:00:47,777
They predict the next token,

25
00:00:47,797 --> 00:00:49,297
one at a time.

26
00:00:49,317 --> 00:00:51,004
Five: the context window.

27
00:00:51,024 --> 00:00:53,537
How much text the model can see at once.

28
00:00:53,557 --> 00:00:55,137
Six: temperature.

29
00:00:55,157 --> 00:00:57,137
Low is focused and repeatable.

30
00:00:57,157 --> 00:00:58,817
High is more varied.

31
00:00:58,837 --> 00:01:00,257
Seven: prompting.

32
00:01:00,277 --> 00:01:01,297
Clear instructions,

33
00:01:01,317 --> 00:01:04,470
examples and a format give better answers.

34
00:01:04,490 --> 00:01:05,617
Eight: few-shot.

35
00:01:05,637 --> 00:01:07,270
Show a few examples in the prompt,

36
00:01:07,290 --> 00:01:09,137
and the model follows the pattern.

37
00:01:09,157 --> 00:01:10,977
Nine: hallucinations.

38
00:01:10,997 --> 00:01:12,497
Answers that sound confident,

39
00:01:12,517 --> 00:01:13,617
but are made up.

40
00:01:13,637 --> 00:01:15,137
Ten: fine-tuning.

41
00:01:15,157 --> 00:01:16,737
Train a model further on your own

42
00:01:16,757 --> 00:01:18,897
examples to change how it behaves.

43
00:01:18,917 --> 00:01:21,137
Eleven: R L H F.

44
00:01:21,157 --> 00:01:22,390
People rank the answers,

45
00:01:22,410 --> 00:01:24,337
and the model learns what they prefer.

46
00:01:24,357 --> 00:01:25,937
Twelve: multimodal.

47
00:01:25,957 --> 00:01:27,697
One model that understands text,

48
00:01:27,717 --> 00:01:29,257
images and audio.

49
00:01:29,277 --> 00:01:30,497
Now the last eight.

50
00:01:30,517 --> 00:01:32,177
This is where the jobs are.

51
00:01:32,197 --> 00:01:34,257
Thirteen: vector databases.

52
00:01:34,277 --> 00:01:35,244
They store embeddings,

53
00:01:35,264 --> 00:01:37,457
and find the closest matches fast.

54
00:01:37,477 --> 00:01:38,897
Fourteen: chunking.

55
00:01:38,917 --> 00:01:40,337
Split long documents into

56
00:01:40,357 --> 00:01:42,017
small pieces you can search.

57
00:01:42,037 --> 00:01:43,457
Fifteen: RAG.

58
00:01:43,477 --> 00:01:45,217
Retrieve the right chunks first,

59
00:01:45,237 --> 00:01:47,537
then answer from them, with sources.

60
00:01:47,557 --> 00:01:49,110
Sixteen: tool calling.

61
00:01:49,130 --> 00:01:50,457
The model asks your code

62
00:01:50,477 --> 00:01:51,377
to run a function,

63
00:01:51,397 --> 00:01:52,737
like a database query.

64
00:01:52,757 --> 00:01:54,230
Seventeen: agents.

65
00:01:54,250 --> 00:01:56,017
A model in a loop that plans,

66
00:01:56,037 --> 00:01:58,257
uses tools and checks its own work.

67
00:01:58,277 --> 00:01:59,804
Eighteen: M C P.

68
00:01:59,824 --> 00:02:01,337
An open standard for plugging

69
00:02:01,357 --> 00:02:03,377
tools and data into AI apps.

70
00:02:03,397 --> 00:02:04,897
Nineteen: evals.

71
00:02:04,917 --> 00:02:06,337
Tests that score the answers,

72
00:02:06,357 --> 00:02:08,577
so you know a change made things better.

73
00:02:08,597 --> 00:02:10,097
Twenty: guardrails.

74
00:02:10,117 --> 00:02:12,230
Checks on what goes in and what comes out,

75
00:02:12,250 --> 00:02:13,937
so the system stays safe.

76
00:02:13,957 --> 00:02:16,657
Tokens to agents: that's the full stack.

77
00:02:16,677 --> 00:02:17,777
Save this, so you have

78
00:02:17,797 --> 00:02:19,797
all twenty in one place.

79
00:02:21,857 --> 00:02:23,997
So, what should I explain next?

80
00:02:24,017 --> 00:02:25,157
Tell me in the comments below,

81
00:02:25,177 --> 00:02:27,377
and it could be the next episode.
